 ### [Uncertainty-Aware SIREN](10449-uncertainty-aware-siren.md)
-Uncertainty-Aware SIREN is a method for estimating prediction confidence in implicit neural representations (INRs) of steady-state vector fields. It addresses the gap where standard neural fields do not quantify prediction confidence, which is critical for downstream scientific decisions. It sets itself apart from its parent [SIREN Neural Field](1250-siren-neural-field.md) by augmenting the architecture with mechanisms to output uncertainty metrics alongside the predicted vector values, and from siblings like [Fourier Positional Encoding](1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md) by focusing on epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty quantification rather than frequency representation.
+Uncertainty-Aware SIREN is a method for estimating prediction confidence in implicit neural representations (INRs) of steady-state vector fields. It addresses the gap where standard neural fields do not quantify prediction confidence, which is critical for downstream scientific decisions. It sets itself apart from its parent [SIREN Neural Field](1250-siren-neural-field.md) by augmenting the architecture with residual blocks and dropout layers to output uncertainty metrics alongside the predicted vector values, and from siblings like [Fourier Positional Encoding](1267-fourier-positional-encoding.md) by focusing on epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty quantification rather than frequency representation.
 
 ### Single Field Fitting
-Single Field Fitting renders single-object fits via Probabilistic Directed Distance Fields (PDDF), requiring a single network evaluation per pixel [S5]. Normals and curvatures are obtained using one or more backward passes for the same oriented point used in the single forward pass [S5]. The implementation uses SIREN MLPs (sine nonlinearity) to allow for higher-order derivatives [S5]. In ablation experiments on the Stanford Bunny, 100K samples of each data type were used, except for the ablated one, with held-out errors measured on 25K samples per type [S5]. Ablating S-type data damages performance across all sample types, likely due to difficulties in knowing when to transition between weight field components [S5]. Ablating T-type data is the most damaging for $L_\xi$-A error, as T-type samples are effectively the hardest subset of A-type data [S5].
